"## Is the Great Wall of China visible from space or from the Moon with the naked eye? What is the verdict of fact-checkers and Wikipedia's account?\n## Short verdict\n\n- **From the Moon: definitively no.** No man-made object — including the Great Wall — is visible to the naked eye from the lunar distance (~384,400 km) [1][2][6].\n- **From low Earth orbit (\"space,\" ~160–400 km): disputed, but generally no.** A few astronauts claim naked-eye sightings under ideal conditions, but the consensus of NASA, fact-checkers, and most astronaut testimony is that the Wall is *not* reliably visible unaided; photos of it require telephoto/zoom lenses [1][2][6].\n- The related claim that it is \"the **only** man-made object visible from space\" is false on both counts — cities, highways, airports, and large structures are far more visible from orbit [1][2][6].\n\n## Why it's physically implausible\n\nThe human eye resolves roughly 1 arcminute (~60 arcseconds). The Wall is only ~5–9 m wide and built of locally sourced earth and stone that blends with the terrain. From the Moon it subtends ~0.005 arcseconds — thousands of times below visual resolution; even from 200 km it subtends only ~10 arcseconds. Contrast, not just size, defeats visibility [1][2][6].\n\n## Key evidence and testimony\n\n- **Apollo astronauts** (e.g., Alan Bean) confirmed nothing man-made is visible from the Moon — only Earth's disk of oceans, continents, and clouds [6].\n- **Yang Liwei**, China's first astronaut (Shenzhou 5, 2003), reported he could **not** see the Wall from orbit; this prompted China's education ministry to announce revisions of school textbooks repeating the claim [7].\n- **Leroy Chiao** (ISS, 2004) photographed a section in Inner Mongolia — but with a 180 mm zoom lens — and said he thought he saw it naked-eye while unsure. NASA's Earth Observatory published the image while noting the Wall is barely distinguishable unaided [2].\n- Astronaut **Ed Lu** called it \"less visible than a lot of other objects\"; **Gene Cernan** claimed the opposite from ~100–200 miles altitude — testimony is conflicting [6].\n- **ESA's cautionary tale:** a 2004 Proba satellite image ESA billed as showing the Wall was later acknowledged to probably show a **river** [8].\n\n## Fact-checkers' verdict\n\n- **Snopes** rates the \"visible from the Moon\" version **False** and treats the blanket \"visible from space\" claim as false/mixed, with low-orbit sightings disputed [3].\n- **AFP** and **Reuters** fact checks conclude the Wall is **not visible from space with the naked eye**; any imagery requires camera zoom [4][5].\n\n## Wikipedia's account\n\nWikipedia's \"Visibility from space\" section on the Great Wall article states the Moon claim is a **myth that predates spaceflight**: the earliest known reference is a **1754 letter by William Stukeley** (writing about Hadrian's Wall, saying the Chinese Wall \"may be discerned at the Moon\"), followed by Henry Norman (1895) and Richard Halliburton (1938), who popularized the \"only man-made object visible from the Moon\" line. The article notes low-orbit visibility is **contested**, cites Yang Liwei's 2003 report and Chiao's 2004 photo, and stresses that many other man-made objects are visible from orbit [1][9].\n\n### Sources\n[1] Wikipedia, \"Great Wall of China\" — Visibility from space section\n[2] NASA Earth Observatory (2004), ISS photo of the Wall by Leroy Chiao, with visibility notes\n[3] Snopes fact check, \"Great Wall of China visible from space/Moon\"\n[4] AFP Fact Check, \"Great Wall not visible from space with the naked eye\"\n[5] Reuters Fact Check on the same viral claim\n[6] NASA statements and astronaut quotes (Ed Lu, Gene Cernan, Alan Bean)\n[7] 2004 reporting (Xinhua/The Guardian) on Yang Liwei's statement and Chinese textbook revisions\n[8] ESA 2004 correction of the Proba image (feature likely a river)\n[9] Stukeley (1754), Norman (1895), Halliburton (1938) — historical origins of the myth\n\n---\n\n## What does the Wikipedia article on the Great Wall of China say about visibility from space and the origin of the myth?\nBased on the Wikipedia article on the Great Wall of China (as reflected in my knowledge of the article; no live fetch was available in this session), here is what it says:\n\n## Visibility from space\n\n- **From the Moon:** The article states it is a myth that the wall is visible from the Moon with the naked eye. It notes the wall's apparent width from the Moon would be comparable to viewing a human hair from about 3 km (2 mi) away, and that seeing it would require eyesight roughly 17,000 times better than normal 20/20 vision.\n- **From low Earth orbit:** The wall is generally *not* visible to the unaided eye, though it is \"barely visible\" under ideal viewing conditions [1]. NASA has made statements to this effect.\n- **Astronaut reports:**\n  - Yang Liwei, China's first astronaut, said after his 2003 Shenzhou 5 flight that he could not see the wall from orbit — which sparked public debate in China and calls to revise school textbooks that repeated the myth [2].\n  - Chinese-American astronaut Leroy Chiao took a photograph from the ISS in 2004 in which the wall could be identified, but he confirmed he had not actually seen it with the naked eye [3].\n  - Astronaut Ed Lu (Expedition 7) said the wall is less visible than many other man-made objects and you have to know exactly where to look.\n- The article notes the wall is hard to spot because it is narrow and built from materials similar to the surrounding terrain, whereas roads, cities, airports, and ships *are* visible from low orbit.\n\n## Origin of the myth\n\nThe article traces the claim through several sources:\n\n1. **William Stukeley (1754)** — in a letter comparing Hadrian's Wall to the Chinese Wall, he wrote that the Great Wall \"makes a considerable figure upon the terrestrial globe, and may be discerned at the Moon\" — one of the earliest recorded statements of the idea [4].\n2. **Henry Norman (1895)** — wrote that the wall \"enjoys the reputation of being the only work of human hands on the globe visible from the moon\" [5].\n3. **Robert Ripley's \"Believe It or Not!\"** cartoons (1930s) repeated the claim, calling it the only man-made work visible from the Moon.\n4. **Richard Halliburton's *Second Book of Marvels* (1938)** — popularized the claim further [6].\n\n**Sources cited by the article:** [1] NASA; [2] Yang Liwei's 2003 statements (Chinese press coverage); [3] Chiao/ISS imagery coverage (e.g., NASA Earth Observatory); [4] Stukeley's 1754 letter; [5] Norman, *The Peoples and Politics of the Far East* (1895); [6] Halliburton, *Second Book of Marvels* (1938).\n\nNote: I could not perform a live retrieval of the current article, so wording and section details may differ slightly from the live Wikipedia page.\n\n---\n\n## Which man-made structures are actually reported as visible from low Earth orbit with the naked eye, according to Wikipedia?\n## Summary (based on Wikipedia's \"Naked eye,\" \"Great Wall of China,\" and related articles)\n\n**The Great Wall myth — debunked.** Wikipedia explicitly states that the popular claim that the Great Wall of China is visible from space with the naked eye is a myth. Chinese astronaut Yang Liwei reported not seeing it, and ISS astronaut Ed Lu said it is *less* visible than many other objects. Skylab astronaut William Pogue initially thought he'd spotted it but realized he was seeing the Grand Canal near Beijing (he could see the Wall only with binoculars). Leroy Chiao's 2004 ISS photos captured it, but only with camera zoom. Wikipedia notes it may be discernible only under ideal conditions (low sun angle, snow cover).\n\n**Structures that ARE reported as naked-eye visible from LEO (~400 km, ISS altitude):**\n- **Cities** — and at night, **city lights**, including major lit highways\n- **Major roads and highways**\n- **Airports**\n- **Dams and reservoirs**\n- **Large ships** (e.g., oil tankers) and their wakes\n- **The Pyramids of Giza** — reported visible by astronauts\n- **The Almería greenhouses, Spain** — the \"Sea of Plastic\" (mar de plástico), cited as strikingly visible from orbit\n- **The Bingham Canyon Mine, Utah** — Wikipedia's article claims it is large enough to be visible to the naked eye from space\n\n**Caveats:** Visibility depends on altitude, sun angle, weather, contrast with surroundings, and knowing where to look. Astronauts generally report that many linear/industrial features are easier to see than the narrow, terrain-colored Great Wall.\n\n*Note: I could not perform a live search in this session; this reflects Wikipedia content as of my knowledge and article titles are cited by name rather than index number.*"